// mlrepro (v-effects, 2026-07-18) — the REMAINING blocker for compiler-ml #4 (real memoized query-DB / Db-state
// effect). The substrate now WORKS on trunk (a two-op Map-state handler folds; a single memoize combinator
// runs; the mlrepro-decline-two-op-map-state-handler-memo-shape PASSES). But a SEQUENCE of two memoizing
// helper calls — the real memo-spine usage, where each query reads/writes the shared Db and a later query must
// see the prior write — FAILS with CDZ0101 "unbound name `v`" (the helper's local `let v` leaks when a second
// sequenced helper-call threads the out-state).
//
// BISECTION (sharp, on trunk):
//   - a SINGLE memoized query in the handle body — get; on-miss (let v=compute in put;v) — RUNS (query(3) → 30) ✅
//   - TWO SEQUENCED queries — (query(3); query(5)) — CDZ0101 unbound `v`  ← this file
// So the trigger is: two sequenced cross-fn calls, EACH performing get+put with a LOCAL let-binding, where the
// out-state threads from the first to the second. This is the v-effects task-#15 / helper-out-state family
// (the deep_fresh_copy / cross-fn out-state-threading seam), NOT a design-blocked area — a bounded fix.
//
// EXPECTED: (query(3); query(5)) runs → 50 (the second query's value; both memoized into the threaded Map).
// ACTUAL: CDZ0101 "unbound name `v`" (the second helper's local `v` resolves against a dead scope after the
// out-state thread). NOT a miscompile (a clean coded reject), but it blocks the memo spine.
//
// v-effects owns this (task-#15 family). Prioritized after #13 B2 lands (B2 = corpus-only, different area).
// compiler-ml #4 is NOT design-blocked — the substrate works; this sequenced-helper fix unblocks the true
// effect-based memo spine. Falls back to threaded-DB (non-effect) if not fixed by the time #4 is reached.

effect Db =
  | get : Int64 -> Option(Int64)
  | put : Tuple(Int64, Int64) -> Unit

def compute(k) = k * 10

def query(k) =
  match Db.get(k) with
  | Option.Some(hit) => hit
  | Option.None(_) => (let v = compute(k) in (Db.put((k, v)); v))

def run() =
  handle Db(Map.empty) with
    | get(k, s) => resume(Map.lookup(s, k), s)
    | put(kv, s) => (match kv with | (k, v) => resume(unit, Map.insert(s, k, v)))
  in
  (query(3); query(5))

@test
def sequenced-memoize() =
  if run() == 50 then unit else trap("query(3);query(5) should thread the Map and return 50")

export { run }

// ── V-EFFECTS ROOT-CAUSE (2026-07-18, dumped the reduced body) ──
// Bisected minimal trigger: a cross-fn helper with a LOCAL LET called TWICE sequenced under a state handler —
//   def store(k) = (let ((v (* k 10))) (do (Db.put (tuple k v)) v))   ; (store 3); (store 5)  → CDZ0101 v
// WITHOUT the local let (put the expr inline) → WORKS. So the `let v` inside the inlined helper is the trigger.
// DUMP of the reduced handle body shows the `let ((v ...))` BINDING IS DROPPED during inline+thread — the put
// arg `(tuple k v)` is left with a BARE `v` (the let-init `(* k 10)` gone), which then either resolves UNBOUND
// or COLLIDES with the handler arm's OWN pattern binder `v` (arm `(put (kv) s (match kv ((tuple k v) ...)))`
// — the arm's `v` shares the name!). So TWO compounding issues: (1) the helper's `let`-init is dropped on the
// inline/thread path (the do/let-collapse family — sibling of the batch-161 beta_reduce do-preservation fix
// 53d95f103, but for a `let`-init referenced by a performed op's ARG, across a SEQUENCED second helper call);
// (2) a NAME COLLISION hazard: the helper's local `v` and the handler arm's pattern `v` are the same name, so
// a dropped/mis-scoped helper `v` can capture against the arm binder. FIX DIRECTION: preserve the inlined
// helper's `let`-init (don't collapse the do/let when it feeds a perform arg) AND ensure deep_fresh_copy /
// the inline re-parenting gives the helper's local `let` binder a FRESH unspellable name so it can't collide
// with an arm binder. Build carefully (state-threading + scope = miscompile-prone); gate: 1-call vs 2-seq,
// with/without local let, helper-v-name = arm-binder-name vs distinct. Task-#15/#11 family. Owner: v-effects.

// ── SHARPENED ROOT-CAUSE (2026-07-18, dumped threaded body of the MINIMAL case) ──
// It is NOT a name collision (rename helper `v`→`vv`: still leaks `unbound vv`). The TRUE minimal trigger:
// a cross-fn helper with a LOCAL `let` inlined as a NON-FINAL `do` item (even ONE call — `(store 3); Db.tot()`
// — leaks; the FINAL-item / whole-body position works). Dump:
//   store body inlined = (let ((vv (* 3 10))) (do (Db.put (tuple 3 vv)) vv))
//   threaded result = ((. Map len) (match (tuple 3 vv) ((tuple k v) ((. Map insert) (Map.empty) k v))))
//   ^ the `let ((vv 30))` WRAPPER IS GONE — `vv` is bare in `(tuple 3 vv)`.
// MECHANISM: the put perform's NEXT-STATE `(Map.insert s k v)` (threaded FORWARD to the later `Db.tot` item)
// AND the substituted scrutinee `(tuple 3 vv)` reference `vv`, which is bound by the helper's `let` LOCAL to
// the performing item. When the perform's out-state threads to the NEXT do-item, it is spliced OUTSIDE that
// `let`'s scope → `vv` escapes → CDZ0101. So: a perform whose ARG references an inlined-helper-local `let`
// binding, in a NON-FINAL do position, loses the binding when its out-state threads forward. Final-position
// works because nothing threads the state past it. FIX DIRECTION: when threading a non-final do item whose
// perform's out-state (or substituted arm body) references a binding LOCAL to that item, the local `let` must
// be HOISTED to wrap the continuation (thread the let-init forward), OR the perform's arg must be let-bound at
// the outer scope before substitution. Sibling of Site-4 (let-init distribution) + the multi-value out-state
// machinery. Task-#15/#11 family. Build carefully; gate 1-call-nonfinal / 2-seq / final-position / distinct
// helper binder name. Owner: v-effects (after #13, which is DONE — both B1+B2 landed).
